    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d332">

                <pb facs="0523" n="423" sameAs="frus:frus1934v02/pg423"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">662.116/212</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The German Ministry for Foreign Affairs to the
                        American Embassy in Germany</hi><anchor n="17" xml:id="d332-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Translation]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. W 2770</seg>
                </opener>
                <p rendition="#center"><hi rendition="#small-caps">Note Verbale</hi></p>
                <p>The Foreign Office has the honor to state to the Embassy of the United States of
                    America—in reply to the latter’s <hi rendition="#italic">note verbale</hi> of
                    April 11, No. 215—the following:</p>
                <pb facs="0524" n="424" xml:id="pg424"/>
                <p>The structure of customs contingents in commercial treaty agreements with other
                    states takes into account primarily the interests of the state with which the
                    customs contingent is agreed upon by treaty. In this connection, the equivalent
                    offered by this state for the granting of the customs contingent is of course of
                    importance.</p>
                <closer>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-left">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Berlin</hi>
                        </placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1934-05-09">May 9,
                            1934</date>.</dateline>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d332-f1" n="17" type="source" xml:id="d332-f1-content">
                <p>Copy transmitted to the Department by the Chargé in Germany in his despatch No.
                    839, May 16; received May 25.</p>
            </note>
